#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""port-sentinel: know every port your server listens on, and get told when that changes.

Reads the kernel socket tables (/proc/net) and checks every listening port against a short
allowlist kept on the server: which ports, which program, how exposed. Anything else is a
problem: a new service bound to 0.0.0.0, a database that left localhost, a port that came back
after you closed it, a required service that stopped listening.

    port-sentinel scan             list listening ports (with their status if an allowlist exists)
    port-sentinel init             write an allowlist from what listens right now
    port-sentinel check            exit 1 on any problem; --notify to alert, --state for cron
    port-sentinel watch            the same, in a loop
    port-sentinel probe HOST       TCP connect scan from outside: what is really reachable

Stdlib only, Python 3.8+. scan/init/check/watch read Linux /proc; probe runs anywhere.
https://github.com/Zodiachz/port-sentinel
"""
import argparse
import datetime
